<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

	<PropertyGroup>
		<!-- The floor and the newest, and nothing between: a parser here is ordinary C#
		     over a generated one, so netstandard2.0 is what every consumer can read and
		     net10.0 is what a consumer on the newest runtime gets without the shims. -->
		<TargetFrameworks>netstandard2.0;net10.0</TargetFrameworks>
		<RootNamespace>DotGram.Web</RootNamespace>

		<Description>Parsers for the formats of the web — JSON, JSON Pointer and Patch, HTTP header fields, URIs and URI Templates, email addresses, timestamps and language tags — written against their specifications and held to their test suites.</Description>
		<PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
		<PackageTags>parser;json;json-patch;uri;uri-template;email;http;http-headers;content-type;link-header;cookie;language-tag;rfc3339;structured-fields;dotgram</PackageTags>
		<PackageReleaseNotes>Since 0.2.0. The same formats, read the same way on accepted input; refusals that cost more than linear time are gone, and a few readers follow their RFC more closely.

BREAKING CHANGES, and what to do

- A Link field is refused where a parameter value breaks the syntax RFC 8288 gives it: a rel or rev that is not relation types separated by spaces, each a registered name or an absolute URI; an anchor that is not a URI-Reference; an hreflang that is not a language tag; a type that is not a type and subtype. Of rel and type only the first is asked. There is no option to accept such a field.
- A Forwarded element read from a position stops earlier, or is refused, where a pair's value is invalid (by, for, host, proto) or a name repeats; a Content-Disposition read from a position refuses a parameter name written twice. Migration: read the whole field (ParseField, TryParseField, Parse, TryParse), which is unchanged.
- An Accept refusal may no longer list Token among what could stand at its position ("Expected Token, ';' or end of input" is now "Expected ';' or end of input"), at the same position.

NEW

- WebLink.ParseStrictField and TryParseStrictField hold a Link field to the ABNF to the letter, and so refuse a registered relation type that is not lowercase (rel=Next), which ParseField takes in any case, as RFC 8288 compares it.

FIXED

- Refusal time. Content-Type, Accept, Forwarded and Link refuse a field with a long run of spaces in linear time, where it was cubic (2.6 seconds for a Content-Type of 1,600 spaces); the Structured Field readers refuse a long key or token in linear time. An email address with an unclosed comment, quoted string or domain literal or a long run of commas, a Content-Type with many empty parameters and a Structured Field Dictionary with many members are refused in linear time, where it was quadratic (1.3 seconds for an address list of 8 KB). Link, Forwarded, Content-Disposition and Accept refuse a long adversarial header in linear time, where each was quadratic. Values, refusals and messages of these are as before.
- Against the RFCs: an IPvFuture host may write its version flag as V (RFC 3986); a URI Template literal refuses the noncharacters at the end of each supplementary plane and U+E0000 to U+E0FFF (RFC 6570); a Set-Cookie Max-Age within a long's range is read exactly, where the values nearest either end came back as long.MaxValue or long.MinValue; a Forwarded field of semicolons alone (;) is a field with no pairs, where it was refused.

CHANGED

- The URI, cookie-date, Content-Disposition, Forwarded and Link readers are carried immediately, as the JSON, JSON Pointer, URI Template, timestamp and Set-Cookie readers already were. Values, refusals and messages are as before; a text refused part-way allocates more.</PackageReleaseNotes>

		<!-- What a caller reads in the editor: every public member of this package has a comment,
		     and a build without one fails here rather than shipping a package that answers nothing
		     to IntelliSense. -->
		<GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>

		<!-- Symbols inside the assembly, as the generator carries them: a stack trace from
		     a parser here has line numbers without a symbol server. -->
		<DebugType>embedded</DebugType>

		<!-- Paths in the PDB and the package normalized to the repository rather than the
		     runner's disk, so that what CI packs can be rebuilt byte for byte from the commit
		     its nuspec names. Here and not in Directory.Build.props: it rewrites every source
		     path, [CallerFilePath] included, to /_/, and nine test classes find the
		     repository from their own file — set for everything, it failed eighty of them.
		     Only on CI, because locally it would point a debugger at /_/. -->
		<ContinuousIntegrationBuild Condition="'$(GITHUB_ACTIONS)' == 'true'">true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild>

		<!-- Generated files land on disk so a reader can open what the grammars became. -->
		<EmitCompilerGeneratedFiles>true</EmitCompilerGeneratedFiles>
		<CompilerGeneratedFilesOutputPath>$(BaseIntermediateOutputPath)GeneratedFiles</CompilerGeneratedFilesOutputPath>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<!-- The generator, and nothing else: a .gram parser acquires no runtime
		     dependency, which is why this can be an ordinary library (docs/syntax.md
		     §6.1). -->
		<ProjectReference Include="..\DotGram\DotGram.csproj"
		                  OutputItemType="Analyzer" ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup Condition="'$(DotGramAnalyzeGenerated)' == 'true'">
		<!-- The generator's own output, analysed for dead or redundant emitted code
		     (tools/DotGram.GeneratedAnalyzers), never packed, loaded only under this
		     property (docs/development.md). -->
		<ProjectReference Include="../../tools/DotGram.GeneratedAnalyzers/DotGram.GeneratedAnalyzers.csproj"
		                  OutputItemType="Analyzer" ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'netstandard2.0'">
		<!-- What a consumer on this framework needs: the generated parser's own methods
		     take `ReadOnlySpan<char>`, which is not in the framework there. It is the one
		     dependency this package has, and it has it only here. -->
		<PackageReference Include="System.Memory" />

		<!-- And what the compilation needs rather than the consumer: the records written
		     by hand beside the grammars have `init` accessors, and `IsExternalInit` is not
		     declared on this framework. The polyfill is a generator and emits it as an
		     internal type, so nothing reaches the package. -->
		<PackageReference Include="Meziantou.Polyfill" PrivateAssets="all" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<PropertyGroup Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'netstandard2.0'">
		<MeziantouPolyfill_IncludedPolyfills>T:System.Runtime.CompilerServices.IsExternalInit;T:System.Index;T:System.Range;T:System.Diagnostics.CodeAnalysis</MeziantouPolyfill_IncludedPolyfills>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<!-- The tests hold the hand parsers to the generated ones, refusals included, and the
		     position a refusal is at is only in the generated Try methods, which are internal. -->
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="DotGram.Tests" />
		<!-- And the trace build's gates, which hold a trace build's refusals to these. -->
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="DotGram.Trace.Tests" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<!-- What the package says for itself. The grammars are in the repository and the
		     README points at them: a parser here is worth reading as much as it is worth
		     calling. -->
		<None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />

		<!-- And the skill beside it, so that an agent in a project that has restored this
		     package can read how to use it without leaving the machine. The README says
		     where it is, in a comment nothing renders. -->
		<None Include="SKILL.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
	</ItemGroup>

</Project>
